   100	  of the specification bytes. [ENC §3.2:265-266]
   101	  - `common.spec.id` other than that value gives `INVALID / SPEC_ID`. The verifier compiles in a **frozen spec
   102	    catalog**; a `common.spec.sha256` not in it gives `UNVERIFIABLE / SPEC_UNKNOWN`. The signed `vectors_sha256` and
   103	    `preamble_sha256` must equal the catalog entry (`INVALID / SPEC_OBJECT_MISMATCH`), and the bundled `spec`,
   104	    `vectors`, `preamble` and `dependency_manifest` bytes must hash to their signed or catalog values
   105	    (`INVALID / BUNDLE_OBJECT_HASH`). Bundled objects are hashed, never parsed as policy or executed. The spec and
   106	    vectors objects are assembled by the part format of section 15.8. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G13]
   107	  - **Comparison targets** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F12]. Bundled spec → signed `common.spec.sha256`; bundled vectors →
   108	    signed `vectors_sha256`; bundled preamble → signed `preamble_sha256`, each independently of catalog lookup
   109	    (`BUNDLE_OBJECT_HASH`). Signed `vectors_sha256` / `preamble_sha256` → catalog entry only if the spec lookup
   110	    succeeds (`SPEC_OBJECT_MISMATCH`). Bundled dependency manifest → catalog entry only if the lookup succeeds;
   111	    otherwise that comparison is unperformable, never compared with an unrelated entry. Other independent failures
   112	    still dominate `SPEC_UNKNOWN`. Bundled objects never become policy because their hashes are self-consistent.
   113	    *(Dated correction 2026-10-14: "must hash to their signed or catalog values" did not say which comparisons
