   212	## 3. Verifier dispatch, stages and precedence
   213	
   214	### 3.1 Dispatch
   215	
   216	1. The verifier reads the bundle's unsigned profile label first. An unrecognized label gives
   217	   `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE`, and nothing else is evaluated. [ENC §8:742-743; A9:272]
   218	2. Out-of-profile layer structure or request gives the same result without evaluation. [DD:70; A9:272]
   219	   Cases c (two-layer transplant) and h (renewal wrapper) land here. [A9:173, 179]
   220	3. **Dispatch reads exactly three things**, in order, and nothing else [OWNER 2026-10-14, G17]:
   221	   - **D1.** The bytes are not strict JSON (ENC §1.4 checks 1–5), or exceed 2^27 bytes: `UNVERIFIABLE /
   222	     OUT_OF_PROFILE`.
   223	   - **D2.** Top-level `profile` absent or not exactly `tessera-v1-strict`: same.
   224	   - **D3.** `layers` not an array of length 1: same (cases c and h).
   225	   - **D4.** The request policy is a **verifier input**, never a bundle member; its only value is `strict`. Any other
   226	     request: `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE`, detail `REQUEST_UNSUPPORTED`, with no evaluation (case d1's waiver
   227	     refused).
   228	   *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` what exactly dispatch inspects … Decide the fields dispatch reads, and where the request
   229	   type lives", 2026-10-13.)*
   230	4. Once dispatched to `tessera-v1-strict`, every codec rejection is `INVALID`. That includes a signed `profile` other
   231	   than `tessera-v1-strict`, an unknown tag and an unknown canonicalization version. [ENC §8:744-746]
   232	5. The one **codec-level** in-profile `UNVERIFIABLE` that concerns the bytes is a well-formed unknown `alg`.
   233	   [ENC §8:747-748; SPEC R-2.37:308] The profile adds further in-profile `UNVERIFIABLE` outcomes (missing members,
   234	   unknown spec hash, binding forms, and the evidence outcomes below); they are not turned `INVALID` by this
   235	   sentence. [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-contra] *(Dated correction 2026-10-14: previously "The one in-profile
   236	   `UNVERIFIABLE` …", repeating ENC §8's stale wording, which G01 already requires to say "codec-level".)*
   237	
   238	### 3.2 Stages
   239	
   240	The stage names follow the spec's. The spec registers no order between stages, only that the verdict does not
   241	depend on evaluation order. [SPEC:1237-1248; SPEC R-5.16:1250] There are ten stages after dispatch. The
   242	table presents ENC/SPEC material; it is not a newly fixed execution order (section 0.1, item 6).
   243	
   244	| Stage | What V1 does | Source |
   245	|---|---|---|
   246	| (a) Parse | Decode every frame, the manifest and `A_sig` under ENC §1 to §5. Reason codes are reported in ENC's order. | [ENC §1.4:172-190; ENC §3.1:241-248; ENC §5:385-394] |
   247	| (b) Type | Tag must be in T and be the expected one: `TAG_UNKNOWN`, `TAG_MISMATCH`. No wrapper stage: one layer only. | [ENC §2:215-218; A9:155] |
   248	| (c) Algorithm | A well-formed unknown `common.alg` gives `UNVERIFIABLE`. A malformed, missing or substituted id gives `INVALID`. | [ENC §3.2:276; SPEC V13-V15:1282-1284] |
   249	| (d) Key binding | Each presented key hashes to its slot's manifest fingerprint (`KEY_MISMATCH`). `manifest_sha256` equals the hash of the manifest frame. | [ENC §5.3:509; ENC §3.2:280] |
   250	| (e) Authority | DNS channel and git channel (section 6), the issuance window, possession proofs. | [A9:137; A9:338-340] |
   251	| (f) Signature set | Both slots are required, from the signed manifest, never from what the bundle presents. Common bytes are byte-equal across slots. | [SPEC V28-V31:1327-1330; ENC §3.4:303-320] |
   252	| (g) Integrity | `SHA256(A_sig)` is the digest the OTS evidence attests. Each committed `S` verifies over its committed `F`. Any difference from the committed tuple gives `RECEIPT_ANCHOR_MISMATCH`; a bad signature gives `SIG_INVALID`. | [ENC §5.3:498-511] |
   253	| (h) Temporal | The three-conjunct predicate at k = 6, plus the T rule (section 7). | [A9:138; SPEC V33-V36:1342-1346; SPEC R-5.19:1352] |
   254	| (i) Revocation | Not evaluated. The report says so. | [A9:248-252] |
   255	| (j) Partition | Section 3.3. | [SPEC R-5.25:1368] |
   256	
   257	- The issuance-window check is part of stage (e) (C4). [A9:338-340; ENC §7.1:636-643]
   258	- **Reported reason.** The reported reason is the first failed check in the **total check order** of section 15.7;
   259	  if none failed, the first unperformable check in that order. The order is a reporting order, not an execution
   260	  order. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G31] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` which reason code is reported when several different
   261	  checks fail", 2026-10-13.)*
   262	- **`DNS_*` record class.** A TXT RRset that is present is run through ENC §7's fifteen-step order; every failure is
   263	  `INVALID` with ENC's code, whether or not DNSSEC was performable. An absent TXT group is `UNVERIFIABLE /
   264	  DNS_EVIDENCE_MISSING`. ENC's `DNS_RRSET` zero case stays a codec vector, unreachable from a V1 container.
   265	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, G24] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` the verdict class of the `DNS_*` record rejections", 2026-10-13.)*
   266	- **Size gates.** An empty snapshot body or a payload over its cap gives `LENGTH_CAP`, before the JSON categories.
   267	  The gates are exact and separate: container 2^27 bytes, payload per ENC §1, snapshot body 2^24. [OWNER 2026-10-14,
   268	  G30] For an empty snapshot body this profile governs over ENC §6's `1 ≤ len` wording and over
   269	  `formal/p8/vectors/gen.py` (`decode_snapshot`, which reports `SCHEMA`): the outcome is `INVALID / LENGTH_CAP`.
   270	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from diff-1, EA-20]
   271	
   272	### 3.3 Precedence and verdicts
   273	
   274	- Order of decision: any required check failed gives `INVALID`; else any required check unperformable gives
   275	  `UNVERIFIABLE`; else `VALID_STRICT`. There is no waiver set, so the degraded branch never applies. [SPEC
   276	  R-5.25:1368; A9:155]
   277	- `INVALID` dominates `UNVERIFIABLE`. [A9:176; SPEC R-5.26:1370; SPEC R-2.33:300] Case d3 depends on it.
   278	- The unperformable rule applies to required checks only. [SPEC R-2.32:298]
   279	- A verdict is never a bare boolean. `UNVERIFIABLE` is never promoted. [SPEC R-2.34:302; SPEC R-5.38:1394]
   280	- No fallback, negotiation or normalization during verification. [SPEC R-5.18:1266; ENC §1.2:156-158]
   281	- **Aggregation.** Physical evaluation order is unrestricted, but reporting uses the fixed total check order (section
   282	  15.7). Early termination is permitted only when every earlier-priority reportable check is settled and every
   283	  stable field, including T, is determined. A failure prevents only checks whose own necessary input cannot be
   284	  obtained; it does not suppress independent receipt-anchor evaluation needed for T. Derived checks are unperformable
   285	  when necessary inputs cannot be obtained, and never pass by default. ~~An unknown `alg` routes the slot as opaque
   286	  (Lemma 7a)~~: signature and possession semantics under an `alg` other than the catalog-supported id are
   287	  unperformable; independent schema and hash checks still run. Codec routing is section 1's codec-known versus
   288	  policy-supported rule. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G26; r2-F01] *Dated correction 2026-10-14: the resolutions draft's "a
   289	  verifier may stop early only on a failed check" is withdrawn; it let two schedules report different stable
   290	  fields. Dated correction 2026-10-14 (r2-F01): "an unknown `alg` routes the slot as opaque" is withdrawn; it
   291	  conflicted with ENC's codec selection for a codec-known but unselected id.*
   292	
   293	### 3.4 Output
   294	
   295	Every report carries these six fields, and they are the stable set that later verifiers must reproduce: the verdict,
   296	its reason code, T, `revocation`, `historical_security` and standing. [A9:156; A9:262-268]
   297	
   298	- `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED`. [A9:248-252]
   299	- `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`. The documentation names what a later examiner must supply: an authenticated
   300	  safe-before cutoff per algorithm and per key, compared with T and A2.1's chain-time uncertainty. [A9:253-259]
   301	- Standing: `ABSENT/NOT_ISSUED_IN_PROFILE`, reported separately and never folded in. [A9:166-167; SPEC R-1.16:163]
   302	- No aggregate checkmark. [SPEC R-5.47:1419]
   303	- **Report encoding.** The stable-output object is the JCS object `{"historical_security":"NOT_EVALUATED",
   304	  "reason","revocation":"NOT_EVALUATED","standing":"ABSENT/NOT_ISSUED_IN_PROFILE","T","verdict"}`; the success
