  1806	### 15.8 Spec and vectors object assembly [OWNER 2026-10-14, G13]
  1807	
  1808	Each object is one or more parts. A part is the ASCII line `tessera-v1-part <path> <length>` LF, then exactly
  1809	`<length>` bytes (decimal, no leading zeros), then LF. `<path>` is repository-relative UTF-8 without spaces. Parts are
  1810	in bytewise-sorted path order; file bytes verbatim as committed. The spec object holds the frozen
  1811	`docs/V1-PROFILE.md` and `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md` (plus the branch-registration supplement under the hybrid). The
  1812	vectors object holds every file under `formal/p8/vectors/` and the non-P8 conformance vectors (RES §L). The vectors
  1813	may embed the spec hash; the spec never embeds the vectors' hash.
  1814	
  1815	**Canonical recipe** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F13]. Supersedes the header and inventory sentences above where they
  1816	differ.
  1817	- **Header grammar.** `tessera-v1-part` SP `<path>` SP `<length>` LF, with fixed single literal spaces. `<path>` is an
  1818	  ASCII repository-relative path matching `[A-Za-z0-9._/-]+`, with no empty, `.` or `..` component, no leading
  1819	  slash, and unique within the object; `<length>` matches `0|[1-9][0-9]*`.
  1820	- **Bytes.** Only tracked regular-file **blob bytes** from a named freeze commit: no newline conversion, no symlink
  1821	  following, no generated caches, no working-tree overlay.
  1822	- **Inventory.** The freeze record publishes the exact sorted path inventory of each object, including the non-P8
  1823	  conformance vector files (RES §L names cases, not paths), with an assembler and an independent byte-agreement
  1824	  check.
  1825	- **A9 is a part (owner choice).** The spec object also carries the governing A9 bytes
  1826	  (`docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md` at its signing commit) as a part, so the object is the complete archival
  1827	  spec; this was chosen over distributing A9 separately with a bound digest.
  1828	- **Hashes live outside.** Actual spec, vectors and catalog hashes are recorded in a separate release/freeze record
  1829	  **outside** the frozen spec parts; recording them inside an included file would be self-reference.
  1830	- The part format is a new packaging choice, not an inherited P8 construction or a proven archival format. Once
  1831	  exact catalog hashes exist, runtime hash checks are unique; runtime never parses these objects as policy.
  1832	  *(Dated correction 2026-10-14: "repository-relative UTF-8 without spaces" admitted LF, CR, tab, absolute and
  1833	  dot paths and duplicates; "every file under formal/p8/vectors/" did not select tracked regular files; "the
  1834	  non-P8 conformance vectors (RES §L)" named cases, not files.)*
  1835	
  1836	### 15.9 Release format [OWNER 2026-10-19, §14.2 item 2]
  1837	
  1838	Source: `docs/V1-FREEZE-OPEN.md` §3 (proposal R1–R6) as adjusted by its "Owner decisions" (2026-10-19); A10.4.2
  1839	(rows 34 and 36); the custody draft `docs/V1-RELEASE-KEY-CUSTODY-DRAFT.md` §2–§3. **Frozen at the 30 Oct freeze:** the
  1840	envelope below (archive, release manifest, signature, TEST/production rules, bootstrap rule). **Not frozen:** the
  1841	verifier's code bytes and its inventory, which each release binds (owner decision 1), and the production key,
  1842	ceremony, bootstrap record and rehearsal, which come after the freeze and before the first public verifier release
  1843	(A10.4.2). None of this is a bundle-verifier rule.
  1844	
  1845	**R1. A release is one archive.**
  1846	- One file, `tessera-verifier-v1-<n>.parts` (production) or `tessera-verifier-v1-TEST-<n>.parts` (TEST); `<n>` is the
  1847	  canonical decimal release number.
  1848	- Its bytes are the section 15.8 part format (header grammar, sorted paths, verbatim tracked regular-file blob bytes
  1849	  at one named **source commit**). Nothing is generated during assembly; the archive is rebuilt from the source commit
  1850	  and compared by hash (the section 15.8 assembler and independent byte-agreement check), which is the input the
  1851	  custody procedure's review-and-build step (A10.4.2 C01) needs.
  1852	- Required parts: `RELEASE.json` (R2); `trust.json`, the exact section 15.5 bytes; `catalog.json`, the exact frozen
  1853	  catalog bytes; `release_constants.py` (R2); the dependency lock (R6); the vendored SHA-1DC source with
  1854	  `SHA1DC-PIN.md`; `install.sh`; the relying-party text; the verifier code. No other part carries authority.
  1855	- The archive is unpacked only after R3 succeeds. Scripts run as `bash install.sh`, so no file mode is needed.
  1856	  *(The custody draft's `.tar` is replaced: the part format has one byte string per file set.)*
  1857	
  1858	**R2. The release manifest and how the trust configuration and catalog bind.**
  1859	- `RELEASE.json` is one closed JCS object with exactly these keys: `format` (`"tessera-v1-release/1"`), `namespace`
  1860	  (`"PRODUCTION"` or `"TEST"`), `release` (= `<n>`), `profile` (`"tessera-v1-strict"`), `source_commit` (40 lowercase
  1861	  hex), `trust_config_sha256`, `trust_config_version`, `catalog_sha256`, `freeze_record_sha256`, `parts` (an object
  1862	  mapping every other part's path to the 64-lowercase-hex SHA-256 of its bytes, exactly the archive's other paths),
  1863	  and `release_key_successor` (40 uppercase hex v4 fingerprint, or null). All values are strings except `parts` (an
  1864	  object of strings) and a null successor. `parts` is how a release binds its verifier code (owner decision 1).
  1865	- **Binding equalities**, checked before signing and again by `install.sh`; any failure means no installation:
  1866	  every `parts` entry equals the hash of that part and the path sets are equal; SHA-256(`trust.json`) =
  1867	  `trust_config_sha256` = `release_constants.TRUST_SHA256`; SHA-256(`catalog.json`) = `catalog_sha256` =
  1868	  `release_constants.CATALOG_SHA256`, and `release_constants.CATALOG_BYTES` equals `catalog.json`; the `version`
  1869	  inside `trust.json` = `trust_config_version`; `release_constants.NAMESPACE` = `namespace`. Under `PRODUCTION`,
  1870	  `catalog_sha256` equals the catalog hash in the freeze record whose SHA-256 is `freeze_record_sha256`.
  1871	- Run time is unchanged: a `--trust` file whose hash is not `TRUST_SHA256`, or that fails section 15.5, is
  1872	  `TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`, no verdict. Each new release's `trust.json` passes the release checker (section 5
  1873	  release invariants, EA-12) against the previous release's.
  1874	- **No machine-specific facts in the signed archive.** `release_constants.py` holds only `NAMESPACE`,
  1875	  `TRUST_SHA256`, `CATALOG_SHA256`, `CATALOG_BYTES` and `UNICODE_VERSION` (`"16.0.0"`), all independent of the build
  1876	  machine. Facts about one installation (the interpreter's `unicodedata` file hash, whether SHA-1DC built) are
  1877	  written by `install.sh` into a per-installation record `install.json`, outside the archive and unsigned; the
  1878	  verifier checks them at start (Python 3.14, Unicode 16.0.0, SHA-1DC present, else the existing startup refusals)
  1879	  and reports the SHA-256 of `install.json` as a diagnostic. `install.json` carries no authority. *(Closes the
  1880	  triage's departure: `build_release.py` wrote `UNICODE_MODULE_SHA256` and `SHA1DC_AVAILABLE` from the build
  1881	  machine into the release.)*
  1882	
  1883	**R3. The release signature.**
  1884	- A **detached, binary (unarmored) OpenPGP v4 signature of type 0x00** over the exact archive bytes, file
  1885	  `<archive>.sig`, ECDSA P-256 with SHA-256, satisfying rule O's grammar and policy (section 8). The signing session
  1886	  checks it against rule O before it leaves the session.
  1887	- The release key is **one v4 ECDSA P-256 primary key with no subkeys**, identified by its full v4 fingerprint. A
  1888	  signature by any other key, or by a subkey, authenticates nothing.
  1889	- The signature covers the whole archive, code included. The key never signs a hash written as text.
  1890	- **Acceptance.** Accept iff the pinned `gpgv` exits 0 on `<archive>.sig <archive>` with a keyring holding **only**
  1891	  the production key whose full fingerprint was obtained by R5; then R2's equalities hold at install.
  1892	  The manifest entry `gpgv` pins the upstream GnuPG 2.4.4 source (digest of the tarball) [OWNER 2026-10-19, item 15; source formal/dependency-sources/MANIFEST.md]. It does not certify
  1893	  distribution builds (the owner machine's Ubuntu `2.4.4-2ubuntu17.6` is patched; its bytes differ). A distribution
  1894	  build is acceptable only if it reproduces the upstream behaviour (exit status) on the release-format test vectors.
  1895	  The version is the one observed; R3/R6 have not yet fixed it, so it remains an owner choice.
  1896	- **TEST releases use this same format** with a TEST key over the whole TEST archive, code included. The
  1897	  `tessera-v1-test-release/1` record (two hashes, raw r‖s, SPKI-hash signer) is withdrawn as a release: it did not
  1898	  cover the verifier code. It survives only as the section 3.5 instrument interface for the conformance and
  1899	  differential harnesses, which is never a release and which an installed verifier refuses (R4). *(Closes the
  1900	  triage's departure on TEST signature coverage.)*
  1901	
  1902	**R4. Production is the default; TEST is explicit.**
  1903	- Every release before the first public verifier release is a **TEST release**: `namespace` `"TEST"`, a `TEST` file
  1904	  name, signed by a TEST release key in the R3 format. TEST keys are throwaway v4 P-256 keys and may live on the
  1905	  operational computer. A TEST public key is never published as the release key, never named in the freeze record,
  1906	  the bootstrap record or relying-party text, and never named in any `release_key_successor` (A10 row 36).
  1907	- The production key signs only `"PRODUCTION"`; a TEST key signs only `"TEST"`. The signing session checks this.
  1908	- **Production refusal is by key, not by label.** A TEST signature fails R3 because the production keyring holds only
  1909	  the production key. The label prevents honest mistakes; it is not a security check.
  1910	- **Mode comes from the installed archive, never from the command line.** There is no `--production` flag. An
  1911	  installed verifier whose `NAMESPACE` is not exactly `"TEST"` is a production verifier: it refuses
  1912	  `--test-release`, `--test-release-key` and every `TESSERA_V1_TEST_*` variable with `TEST_AUTHORITY_REFUSED` and
  1913	  no verdict, whatever else the command line or environment says. Omitting a flag can therefore never select TEST.
  1914	- **The explicit TEST provisioning path** is one of: (a) an installed TEST archive (`NAMESPACE = "TEST"`),
  1915	  acquired by R3 against a TEST keyring the operator names explicitly; or (b) the section 3.5 instrument interface,
  1916	  usable only from a source checkout with no installed release, where the TEST release, TEST key and catalog must
  1917	  all be supplied; with none supplied it refuses (`TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`). *(Closes the triage's departure:
  1918	  production mode was a CLI flag, so an invocation without it accepted TEST provisioning.)*
  1919	- **Rehearsal (A10 row 36), before the first public verifier release:** an otherwise correct archive signed only by
  1920	  a TEST key is refused by the R3/R5 acquisition procedure, including when the TEST public key is offered as the
  1921	  key; and an installed production verifier refuses the section 3.5 interface.
  1922	
  1923	**R5. Out-of-band authentication of the production key** (owner decision 2).
  1924	- The relying party's root of trust is the production key's **full v4 fingerprint**, obtained before and apart from
  1925	  any release. A key or fingerprint that arrives with a release, on the same page as a release, or from the
  1926	  operational computer (the NUC), this repository, DNS or the issuing AWS account authenticates nothing.
  1927	- **Frozen rule:** the fingerprint is published through **at least two channels independent of the operational
  1928	  environment**, named in the custody record before the first public verifier release (A10.4.2). The channels are
  1929	  the author's to name. *(Owner recommendation for then, not frozen: a Zenodo record under the author's ORCID plus
  1930	  one offline printed copy.)* A relying party who did not obtain the fingerprint this way relies on authenticated
  1931	  first acquisition as an assumption, which the relying-party text states (A10.4.2: "does not protect a user who …
  1932	  acquires a substituted initial key").
  1933	- The fingerprint is recorded in the dated **first-production-release bootstrap record**, which names the freeze
  1934	  record by hash and the production key, and is published and OpenTimestamps-stamped. Publication and stamping give
  1935	  a dated public record; they are not authentication. The freeze record does not contain the fingerprint.
  1936	- **Rotation.** A new release key is introduced only by a production release, signed by the current key, whose
  1937	  `release_key_successor` names it; the next release is signed by the successor.
  1938	- **Loss or compromise.** V1 has no assured recovery. New releases stop; a replacement key needs new out-of-band
  1939	  publication under this rule and a new dated bootstrap record. Genuine old releases stay available.
  1940	
  1941	**R6. Dependency pinning.**
  1942	- The archive carries a lock with exact versions **and SHA-256 hashes** for every Python package, installed with
  1943	  hash checking required; wheels are retrieved at install time, not distributed.
  1944	- Python 3.14 with Unicode 16.0.0 tables is checked at install and at start; SHA-1DC is built from the vendored,
  1945	  digest-checked source.
  1946	- The relying party's `gpgv` version and the OpenPGP standard it implements are dependency-manifest entries
  1947	  (section 9; section 14.2 item 15). Verification is offline after installation; installation is not.
  1948	
  1949	**Instrument work this implies (not frozen text):** the e2e `release` stage moves its TEST releases to R1–R3 with a
  1950	TEST key; `verify.py` drops `--production` and takes its mode from `release_constants.NAMESPACE`; `build_release.py`
  1951	stops writing machine facts; `install.sh` writes `install.json`; the rehearsal's acquisition half is new.
# The service-key listing is cut from V1 (A10 row 33)

> **OWNER DECISION, 2026-10-19** (Claude Opus 5.5, owner instance). GATE action that the registered text already
> provides: A10 row 33 makes the listing the second item cut and states what applies when it is cut. No amendment.
> The author adopted option (e) on 2026-10-18, including row 33. He can override this cut until the freeze assembly
> (26 Oct, `docs/V1-EXIT-30OCT.md`); after that, putting the listing back means a new spec object.

## What is cut

A10.4.1 and the rows that take effect only under option (e) with it: 25–32 and 37–39. These are the `service_keys`
trust-configuration member, its classifier and reason codes, the listing pre-commitment, the C0/C1 bootstrap, and
the listing-evidence package that relying parties would have to keep outside the bundle.

## What applies instead (row 33)

A10.4 option (a): honest signed material stays protected under authority capture (rows 15–16; contract G10). **V1
gives no protection against new receipts under a captor's own keys** when both authority channels are captured, and
the relying-party text and the freeze record say so. Release-key custody (A10.4.2) is unchanged: the cut does not
cure a failure of release-key independence, and the custody gate at the first public verifier release still holds.

## Why

1. **Schedule.** The 30 Oct exit audit (`docs/V1-EXIT-30OCT.md`) puts the listing on the critical path, with about
   one working day of slack. Cutting it saves about 4 days (owner estimate). The first cut item, the PQ hybrid, is
   already registered and would save almost nothing.
2. **What it buys is narrow.** It protects only relying parties whose verifier release is authentic. After review
   r4 its protection against a later release-key theft needs independently authenticated Bitcoin history. It moves
   trust from two credentials (DNS and repository key) to a third (the release key), and all three have the same
   custodian.
3. **What it costs relying parties.** A listing-evidence package outside the receipt bundle that holders must keep
   (contract review VC-02). This is the one mechanism added purely for a decades-later adjudicator
   (`docs/decisions/2026-10-19-horizon-cost.md`), and it is the one the author asked about and got no clear answer to.

## What is lost, honestly

- Production receipts issued before any later listing can never have its protection. A listing added in a later
  version protects only receipts after it.
- The horizon analysis recommended keeping the listing at its signed scope, on that ground. The owner weighs the
  schedule and the relying-party burden above it for V1.

## Registered as a candidate, not an obligation

A key listing with a chain-order pre-commitment, or a witnessed log of key assertions, is a candidate for a later
version. Neither creates an obligation.

