   228	
   229	## 2. The tagged byte strings: one header, four tags
   230	
   231	Every byte string that a V1 slot key signs (attestation and manifest frames), and every receipt-anchor and snapshot
   232	preimage that OTS stamps, begins with the same header. Two V1 byte strings carry no header: the git channel's
   233	stamped message, which is exactly the 40 lowercase hex digits of the signed commit id followed by one LF (A9.5,
   234	§7.2), and the key-publication value (§7), which the repository key and the DNSSEC keys sign through git and DNS.
   235	Neither is a frame (Remark 10 of PROOF.md). [OWNER 2026-10-19, Levadura C6] *(Was: "Every V1 byte string that a V1
   236	key signs or that OTS stamps begins with the same header:", which the git stamped message contradicts.)*
   237	
   238	The header:
   239	
   240	```
   241	header(t) = u8 len(t) || t                       t ∈ T (ASCII, no NUL)
   242	```
   243	
   244	| Tag `t` | len | First bytes (hex) | Construction |
   245	|---|---|---|---|
   246	| `tessera-v1/attest` | 17 | `11 74 65 73 …` | frame, §3 |
   247	| `tessera-v1/manifest` | 19 | `13 74 65 73 …` | frame, §4 |
   248	| `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig` | 29 | `1d 74 65 73 …` | signature-inclusive anchor preimage `A_sig`, §5 |
   249	| `tessera-v1/dnssec-snapshot` | 26 | `1a 74 65 73 …` | snapshot preimage, §6 |
   250	
   251	`tessera-v1/receipt-anchor` (25, `19 …`), the frame-only anchor tag of the 2026-10-09 draft, is **withdrawn**. It
   252	is not in T, so it is rejected with `TAG_UNKNOWN` (§5.1).
   253	
   254	- One string serves as both the domain-separation tag and the object type. R-3.11 lists the two as separate items,
   255	  but A1 P8's four-field frame has a single slot. The prefix `tessera-v1/` does the domain separation, and the
   256	  suffix names the type (G-07 resolved).
   257	- A tag outside T is rejected with `TAG_UNKNOWN`. That includes P7 types V1 does not use (`tessera-v1/wrapper`)
   258	  and later versions (`tessera-v2/attest`). A tag in T that is not the expected one is rejected with
   259	  `TAG_MISMATCH`. *Reason: these are the registered "type-tag mismatch" rejections, and the tag decides the
   260	  construction before any other byte is read.*
   261	- The length byte makes T prefix-free as headers. The proof's Lemma 4 depends on that.
   262	- *Why ASCII tags:* a reader with no V1 software can see them in a hex dump. That helps C6.
   263	
   264	---
   265	
   266	## 3. The attestation frame (one per signer)
   267	
   268	### 3.1 Layout (the four registered fields, A1 P8 / A7 §A7.1)
   269	
   270	```
   271	frame(t, p) = header(t) || u16 canonicalization_version || u32 payload_length || payload
   272	            with canonicalization_version = 0x0001 and payload_length = len(payload) ≤ 2^24
   273	```
   274	
   275	| Offset | Width | Field | Value / rule | Reason |
   276	|---|---|---|---|---|
   277	| 0 | 1 | tag length | `0x11` for attest | prefix-free tags |
   278	| 1 | 17 | `type_tag` | `tessera-v1/attest` | §2 |
   279	| 18 | 2 | `canonicalization_version` | `0x0001` means RFC 8785 + §1's domain + §1.2's number rule. Any other value: `CV_UNKNOWN` | fixed width; G-02 resolved (u16, value 1) |
   280	| 20 | 4 | `payload_length` | the exact payload length, ≤ 2^24, else `LENGTH_CAP` | G-01 changed from u64 to u32. Four bytes cover the cap, and a smaller field is one less thing to get wrong |
   281	| 24 | L | `payload` | `JCS(value)`, §3.2 | A7 §A7.1: the canonical payload bytes |
   282	
   283	The frame ends **exactly** at offset 24 + L.
   284	- If fewer than L payload bytes remain, the result is `TRUNCATED`. Truncation inside the header also gives
   285	  `TRUNCATED`.
   286	- If more remain, the result is `TRAILING`.
   287	
   288	The registered rejections "length-prefix mismatch" and "trailing bytes" are therefore one condition, reported by
   289	its direction. Once the length is the only payload boundary, a short length and a trailing byte are the same
   290	event.
   291	
   292	### 3.2 The attestation payload
   293	
   294	The payload is exactly this object. **No member may be missing or added** (`SCHEMA`). *Reason: a closed schema
   295	means no unsigned or unknown field can carry meaning, which is R-3.39.*
   296	
   297	```
   298	{"common":{
   299	   "alg":                 A,     ^[A-Z0-9][A-Z0-9-]{0,63}$          V1 issues the catalog id (see below)
   300	   "binding_forms":       [F…],  nonempty, strictly ascending, each ^[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]{0,31}$
   301	   "content":             <any value in V>                          the requester's content
   302	   "declared_issue_time": "YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ"                    a real UTC date and time, seconds 00–59
   303	   "issuer":              D,     lowercase LDH name, ≥ 2 labels, labels 1–63, total ≤ 241
   304	   "manifest_sha256":     hex64  hex(SHA256(manifest frame)), §4
   305	   "preamble_sha256":     hex64  hex(SHA256(purpose-preamble bytes))
   306	   "profile":             "tessera-v1-strict"                       exact
   307	   "spec":{"id": S,  ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._/-]{0,127}$                  V1: "tessera-v1-strict/verification-spec/1"
   308	           "sha256": hex64}      hex(SHA256(specification bytes))
   309	   "vectors_sha256":      hex64  hex(SHA256(conformance-vector bytes))
   310	 },

exec
/bin/bash -lc "nl -ba formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md | sed -n '310,602p'" in /home/tony/projects/tessera
 succeeded in 0ms:
   310	 },
   311	 "signer":{"key_fp": FP}}        FP ∈ §3.3
   312	```
   313	
   314	`hex64` means exactly 64 **lowercase** hex digits. Members appear in JCS order, which is the order shown.
   315	
   316	- **The id V1 issues.** Under the registered hybrid branch (A9.6; HYBRID-SUPPLEMENT §1, registered 2026-10-16), V1
   317	  issues the pair id `KMS-MLDSA65-PURE-GPG-OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256` (§5.2). The classical id
   318	  `OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256` stays codec-known (rule O in both slots) and is policy-unsupported in the V1
   319	  installation: `UNVERIFIABLE / ALG_UNKNOWN` (V1-PROFILE §1, §8). [OWNER 2026-10-19, Levadura C2] *(Was: `V1 issues
   320	  "OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256"`, the fallback branch's id; the supplement's superseded-statement list omitted it.)*
   321	- **Field grammars** [OWNER 2026-10-19, G18; codifies what both codecs already do, no vector change].
   322	  `declared_issue_time`: years 0001–9999, proleptic Gregorian, seconds 00–59. `issuer`: labels match
   323	  `[a-z0-9-]{1,63}` with no leading or trailing hyphen; all-numeric labels, the top label included, are allowed; at
   324	  least two labels; at most 241 bytes in total.
   325	
   326	| Field | Reason, and the G-id it resolves |
   327	|---|---|
   328	| `alg`: any well-formed id, not only V1's | P8 accepts a well-formed unknown id, and stage (c) returns `UNVERIFIABLE`. The canary (R-3.53) needs this, and G-10b's failure mode is avoided. The id names the scheme: "a v4 OpenPGP binary-document signature whose document is the frame, ECDSA P-256, SHA-256" (G-57 kept). Its location inside the payload is A3 §A3.6.1's. G-11 is resolved for V1. |
   329	| `binding_forms`: a sorted, unique, nonempty array | R-3.37 says P8 "defines the set's canonical encoding, ordering, uniqueness and bounds". It sits in the attestation's signed bytes (G-17 kept). *Which* forms V1 requires is C4 profile work. The vectors use `["STMT_DIRECT"]` for illustration. |
   330	| `declared_issue_time`: second precision, `Z` | One spelling per instant. No leap second, because Unix time and Bitcoin time have none. G-16 kept. |
   331	| `issuer` = D, at most 241 characters | D is bound in the signed bytes for the owner-name check (C4, G-21). 241 keeps `_tessera-v1.<D>` within DNS's 255-octet name limit (§7). |
   332	| `manifest_sha256` over the manifest **frame** | The hash covers a tagged string, so it is domain-separated by construction. G-14 is resolved: the hash covers the frame of the tuple and nothing more. |
   333	| `spec`, `vectors_sha256`, `preamble_sha256` | R-3.50: the spec id and hash are in the signed bytes. The vectors and preamble are bound the same way. Each hash is over the object's raw bytes, so `sha256sum` reproduces it with no Tessera code. G-18 and G-47 are resolved as far as P8 goes: they are bound. Consuming them is verifier work. |
   334	| `profile` inside `common` | The signed bytes name their own profile (C6). Dispatch reads the bundle's unsigned profile label first (§8). |
   335	| `content` is arbitrary V | Any JSON value in V. A bare digest string is accepted (C-06 and F-03). |
   336	
   337	### 3.3 Fingerprint slot encodings (G-10 resolved; owner ruling for the DNS record)
   338	
   339	| Slot | `key_fp` string | Definition |
   340	|---|---|---|
   341	| `kms` | `spki-sha256:` + 64 hex | hex(SHA256(DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo)). For P-256 the SPKI is the 26-byte prefix `3059301306072a8648ce3d020106082a8648ce3d030107034200` followed by `04‖X‖Y` (91 bytes). This is what KMS `GetPublicKey` returns. |
   342	| `gpg` | `gpg-sha256:` + 64 hex | hex(SHA256(body)), where `body` is the v4 public-key packet body (RFC 9580 §5.5.2): `04` ‖ u32 creation ‖ `13` ‖ `08 2a8648ce3d030107` ‖ u16 `0x0203` ‖ `04‖X‖Y`. These are exactly the bytes that the v4 fingerprint hashes *after* its `0x99` ‖ u16 length prefix. That prefix is **not** included, and SHA-1 is not used. This is **not** the fingerprint gpg prints; a tool computes it from the exported key packet. |
   343	
   344	- The scheme prefix makes the two slots' strings differ in their first byte, so they can never be equal. It also
   345	  makes the frame self-describing.
   346	- The DNS record (§7) carries the bare hex under the slot name. The verifier compares `kms=<h>` with
   347	  `spki-sha256:<h>` and `gpg=<h>` with `gpg-sha256:<h>`.
   348	- *Consequences to record:*
   349	  - V1 uses no SHA-1 for key binding. The gpg slot binds the whole key packet body, creation time included, under
   350	    SHA-256.
   351	  - The KMS key's OpenPGP key-packet creation time is **not** covered by its `spki-sha256` fingerprint. This is
   352	    **accepted**: for the KMS slot the verifier binds the SPKI (the key KMS actually holds), not the OpenPGP key
   353	    packet. The OpenPGP wrapper's creation time therefore carries no meaning for that slot.
   354	
   355	### 3.4 The two frames, as bytes (A5 §A5.4 and R-3.12 stated over bytes)
   356	
   357	For one receipt with common value `C`:
   358	
   359	```
   360	F_kms = 11 'tessera-v1/attest' 0001 u32(L_kms) '{"common":' JCS(C) ',"signer":{"key_fp":' JCS(fp_kms) '}}'
   361	F_gpg = 11 'tessera-v1/attest' 0001 u32(L_gpg) '{"common":' JCS(C) ',"signer":{"key_fp":' JCS(fp_gpg) '}}'
   362	```
   363	
   364	- `JCS(C)` occupies bytes **[34, 34 + |JCS(C)|)** of both frames and is byte-identical. That covers the requester
   365	  content and every common binding.
   366	- The frames differ only in bytes 20–23 (the length) and in the `signer` member. In F-01/F-02 they first differ
   367	  at offset 23, then from offset 745, and the lengths differ by 1 byte (the prefixes `spki-sha256:` and
   368	  `gpg-sha256:`).
   369	- Payload-byte identity of `common` is therefore **not** frame equality. The two frames are never equal (C1).
   370	- No slot name is written: A5 §A5.4 says only the fingerprint differs. The slot comes from the manifest's order:
   371	  `key_fp` is slot `kms` and `sset[0]` is slot `gpg` (G-09 kept). A frame in the wrong slot gives
   372	  `RECEIPT_SLOT`.
   373	
   374	### 3.5 What exactly is signed (OpenPGP trailer, PHASE3 finding 4)
   375	
   376	Under `alg = OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256`, each slot's signature is an OpenPGP v4 signature of **type 0x00
   377	(binary document)** whose document is the frame. Its exact byte serialization is fixed by §5.2, rule O. A raw
   378	FIPS 204/205 signer signs the frame itself (§5.2, rule R). For OpenPGP, the signed hash input is
   379	`frame ‖ trailer`, where:
   380	
   381	```
   382	trailer = 04 ‖ 00 ‖ pubalg ‖ hashalg ‖ u16 hlen ‖ hashed-subpackets ‖ 04 FF ‖ u32(6 + hlen)
   383	```
   384	
   385	- The trailer's last four bytes give `6 + hlen`, the length of its hashed header (`04 ‖ 00 ‖ pubalg ‖ hashalg ‖
   386	  u16 hlen ‖ hashed-subpackets`); the whole trailer is `12 + hlen` bytes, that value plus six. So `frame ‖ trailer`
   387	  splits into frame and trailer reading from the end, and the frame's own `payload_length` then delimits the
   388	  payload. PROOF.md Lemma 9 covers this. [OWNER 2026-10-19, P8F-05] *(Was: "The trailer's last four bytes give its
   389	  own length … PROOF.md Lemma 7 covers this.")*
   390	- **Signature type 0x01 (text) must be rejected:** text mode rewrites line endings, and frame headers and lengths
   391	  can contain the bytes `0x0a` and `0x0d`.
   392	- The unhashed subpacket area carries no meaning. Its exact bytes are nonetheless committed by `A_sig` (§5), as
   393	  are all of the signature's bytes.
   394	- **Identifier rule (W1, revised 2026-10-12, finding 5).** No V1 semantic, standing or attempt identifier is
   395	  derived from signature bytes. The anchor digest `SHA256(A_sig)` does commit to them, by design. It is an
   396	  existence commitment for this receipt's exact bytes, not an identifier of the statement (§8).
   397	- **Malleability now means exact-copy integrity, not forgery.** A signature can be changed and still verify:
   398	  - ECDSA's `(r, n − s)`;
   399	  - an edited unhashed subpacket;
   400	  - another packet serialization;
   401	  - a fresh randomized signature over the same frame.
   402	
   403	  Any such change gives bytes that fail the stamped anchor (`RECEIPT_ANCHOR_MISMATCH`, negative controls XR-10
   404	  to XR-14). So none of them lets a new signature inherit an old time bound.
   405	  - What remains is copy integrity. A mirror that alters a signature byte damages its copy, just as altering a
   406	    frame byte would. Preservation must keep the committed bytes exactly.
   407	  - A later re-stamp of an altered copy gets a new, later time bound. It does not restore the original one.
   408	  - V1 does not require signatures to be unique, and it normalizes no signature (low-s or otherwise). Low-s would
   409	    not cover OpenPGP serialization or unhashed-area changes, and any normalization after stamping would break the
   410	    anchor. Serialization choices are made before stamping (§5.4).
   411	
