   413	
   414	## 4. The manifest frame (map-v1 tuple; G-08, G-14, G-22)
   415	
   416	```
   417	F_manifest = frame("tessera-v1/manifest", JCS({"alg":A,"issuer":D,"key_fp":fp_kms,"sset":[fp_gpg],"ver":"map-v1"}))
   418	manifest_sha256 = hex(SHA256(F_manifest))
   419	```
   420	
   421	- The schema is closed: `key_fp` must be a `spki-sha256:` string, and `sset` must hold exactly one `gpg-sha256:`
   422	  string. `ver` is exactly `map-v1`. Anything else is `SCHEMA`.
   423	- *Reason: this is the map-v1 tuple of R-3.36 in the slot order of the C-C2 trace (G-09). Each key's possession
   424	  proof (R-3.38) is its OpenPGP signature over this same frame. The `tessera-v1/manifest` tag keeps that
   425	  signature apart from the same key's attestation signature (P7).*
   426	
   427	## 5. The receipt-anchor preimage `A_sig` (G-19 and G-59 resolved; K-2; revised 2026-10-12)
   428	
   429	```
   430	A_sig = header("tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig") ‖ u8 0x02
   431	        ‖ u32 len(F_kms) ‖ F_kms ‖ u32 len(S_kms) ‖ S_kms
   432	        ‖ u32 len(F_gpg) ‖ F_gpg ‖ u32 len(S_gpg) ‖ S_gpg
   433	anchor digest = SHA256(A_sig), the value OpenTimestamps stamps
   434	```
   435	
   436	- `S_x` is slot x's detached signature over `F_x`, exactly as §5.2 serializes it.
   437	- Slots appear in **manifest slot order**: `kms` is the manifest's `key_fp`, and `gpg` is `sset[0]`.
   438	- The count byte is the number of slots. It must be 2 (`ANCHOR_COUNT`).
   439	
   440	**Decoding, slot by slot.** Each step's boundary is fixed by bytes already read.
   441	1. `u32` frame length. Above 2^24 + 64 gives `LENGTH_CAP`. Then exactly that many bytes, decoded as one complete
   442	   attestation frame (§3.1). That decode yields the frame's `common.alg`.
   443	2. `u32` signature length. 0 gives `SIG_EMPTY`. Above the cap of the rule for `(common.alg, slot)` gives
   444	   `LENGTH_CAP`.
   445	3. Exactly that many bytes, decoded by that rule (§5.2).
   446	4. After the second signature, any byte gives `TRAILING`. A slot running past the end of input gives
   447	   `TRUNCATED`.
   448	
   449	The rule for a slot is chosen from that slot's own frame and its position, both already read.
   450	
   451	*Reason.* One OTS proof must cover a receipt that has two signed frames (R-3.42 with reading (a)). It must commit
   452	to the signatures, not only the frames: a stamp over unsigned frames proves only that the frames existed. It
   453	leaves open signing them later, once signing capability for every required slot has been obtained (CODEX-REVIEW
   454	finding 1; finding 2 for "every slot"). Length prefixes and whole, exactly consumed parts make the preimage
   455	injective without any hash assumption (PROOF Lemma 7). The tag keeps it from being taken for a snapshot's proof.
   456	
   457	**Outside the preimage.**
   458	- The outputs of anchoring: the OTS proof, txid, block headers and confirmation evidence. Including them would
   459	  be circular (finding 4).
   460	- Transport decoration: armor, containers and the bundle format.
   461	
   462	### 5.1 Why the tag changes (and the count byte does not)
   463	
   464	The 2026-10-09 frame-only layout used the tag `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor`. That tag is **withdrawn and removed
   465	from T**. A frame-only preimage is therefore rejected at its header with `TAG_UNKNOWN` (XA-09). A fully signed
   466	receipt presented with a frame-only anchor is rejected the same way (XR-09).
   467	
   468	There is **no fallback**. No verifier accepts a frame-only anchor under any profile label.
   469	
   470	Reasons:
   471	- **Separation by construction.** With a distinct tag, rejecting the old layout is PROOF Lemma 4: the header
   472	  alone decides it. It does not depend on whether bytes in the old layout happen to fail a signature rule.
   473	  - Under the old tag, that dependence would be real. Under rule R, any byte string of the right length is a
   474	    syntactically valid signature.
   475	  - XA-10 shows the same-tag case: the old layout is rejected today only because the gpg frame's first byte
   476	    fails rule O's packet header.
   477	- **Readable dumps.** A hex dump names which construction it holds (C6).
   478	- **No migration cost.** Nothing was issued under the draft layout, so replacing it costs nothing.
   479	
   480	The count byte keeps its meaning (two slots), so it is not the place to carry the version.
   481	
   482	### 5.2 Signature-slot rules (by algorithm identifier)
   483	
   484	Each slot's `S` is **exactly one detached signature** in the single serialization its rule pins.
   485	- `S` is **nonempty** and **at most the rule's cap**.
   486	- The decoder **consumes `S` exactly**. Bytes beyond the signature's own extent give `TRAILING`, which covers a
   487	  second packet. Fewer bytes give `TRUNCATED`.
   488	- **Never `S`:** ASCII armor, a display string, a keyring, or any container that can hold more than one
   489	  signature.
   490	
   491	The rule is chosen by `(common.alg, slot)`:
   492	
   493	| `common.alg` | slot `kms` | slot `gpg` | Cap per `S` |
   494	|---|---|---|---|
   495	| `OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256` (the classical V1 signers) | rule O | rule O | 1024 bytes |
   496	| `KMS-MLDSA65-PURE-GPG-OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256` (hybrid pair id; registered by the branch-registration supplement of 2026-10-16, A9.6) | rule R: ML-DSA-65 (FIPS 204), pure, empty context | rule O (OpenPGP P-256; slot-2 custody per A9.6) | `kms`: exactly 3309; `gpg`: 1024 |
   497	| any other well-formed id (§3.2) | opaque | opaque | 1 to 65536 bytes; no inner rule. Stage (c) gives `UNVERIFIABLE` (canary, A-02) |
   498	
   499	**Rule O (OpenPGP v4 signature, ECDSA P-256, SHA-256; RFC 9580 §4.2, §5.2.3).**
   500	
   501	*The packet header (`SIG_PACKET`).*
   502	- `S` is exactly one Signature packet (tag 2) with a **legacy-format header in its shortest form**:
   503	  - `0x88 ‖ u8 n` when the body has n ≤ 255 bytes;
   504	  - `0x89 ‖ u16 n` when 256 ≤ n ≤ 1021.
   505	- Each of these gives `SIG_PACKET`:
   506	  - any other first byte: an OpenPGP-format `0xC2` header, another packet tag, or ASCII armor's `-`;
   507	  - a longer length form than needed.
   508	- This also excludes partial and indeterminate lengths.
   509	
   510	*The packet body (`SIG_FORMAT`).* The body is, in order:
   511	1. version `04`, type `00`, public-key algorithm `13` (19, ECDSA), hash algorithm `08` (SHA-256);
   512	2. `u16` hashed length, then the hashed subpackets;
   513	3. `u16` unhashed length, then the unhashed subpackets;
   514	4. two bytes: the left 16 bits of the digest;
   515	5. MPI r;
   516	6. MPI s.
   517	
   518	Each of the following holds, or the result is `SIG_FORMAT`:
   519	- each subpacket area is an exact sequence of subpackets, each at least 1 byte, with shortest-form lengths. The
   520	  length forms are RFC 9580 §5.2.3.7's: first octet < 192, one octet; 192–254, two octets
   521	  `((o1−192)<<8)+o2+192` (192–16319); 255, then a four-octet big-endian value (five octets in all). The length
   522	  counts the type octet and is ≥ 1. A longer form than needed is `SIG_FORMAT`; this is a V1 rule, not an RFC ban.
   523	  [OWNER 2026-10-19, G28];
   524	- each MPI is 1 to 256 bits, with an exact bit count;
   525	- the body ends exactly after s.
   526	
   527	Type `0x01` (text) is `SIG_FORMAT` (D-10). The codec does not interpret subpacket contents.
   528	
   529	*Reasons.*
   530	- **The legacy header is what the gpg signer emits.** GnuPG 2.4.4 emits exactly this header for a detached
   531	  P-256 signature: `88 75 04 00 13 08 …`, a 119-byte packet, observed 2026-10-12. Its output is therefore `S`
   532	  unchanged.
   533	  - The KMS slot's packet is assembled by Tessera's own code, and it must emit the same form.
   534	  - A tool that emits an OpenPGP-format header must have its output re-framed **before stamping**. The header is
   535	    not covered by the signature hash, so re-framing does not affect validity.
   536	  - A verifier never re-frames.
   537	- **The cap.** 1024 bytes is about 8× a GnuPG signature, and it still bounds the malleable unhashed area.
   538	
   539	**Rule R (raw FIPS 204 / FIPS 205 signature).**
   540	- `S` is the raw signature byte string that the standard's signing algorithm outputs (the FIPS 204 `sigEncode`
   541	  output; the FIPS 205 signature). It has no header and no wrapper.
   542	- Its length is **exactly** the parameter set's signature size. Any other nonzero length gives `SIG_FORMAT`.
   543	- The signed message is the frame itself (`F_manifest` for a possession proof). No trailer, no transformation.
   544	- **Registered pair (branch-registration supplement of 2026-10-16, A9.6).** `KMS-MLDSA65-PURE-GPG-OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256`:
   545	  slot `kms` is ML-DSA-65 (FIPS 204) in pure mode with the empty context, so the FIPS 204 message representative is
   546	  `0x00 ‖ 0x00 ‖ F`; slot `gpg` is rule O. `S_kms` is exactly 3309 bytes. Length 0 gives `SIG_EMPTY`, above 3309
   547	  `LENGTH_CAP`, any other length `SIG_FORMAT`. The codec reads no byte of `S_kms`: a malformed hint encoding or an
   548	  out-of-range `z` is a verification failure (`SIG_INVALID`; `POSSESSION_INVALID` for possession), not a codec code.
   549	- The issuer obtains `S_kms` (attestation and possession) from KMS with `MessageType EXTERNAL_MU` only, sending
   550	  μ = SHAKE256(SHAKE256(pk, 64) ‖ 0x00 ‖ 0x00 ‖ F, 64), pk the raw 1952-byte key (RFC 9881 Appendix D). The result is
   551	  an ordinary pure signature over `F`. The verifier never sees μ and verifies over `F`.
   552	- The kms key under this id is the exact DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo with OID 2.16.840.1.101.3.4.3.18 and no parameters
   553	  (22-byte prefix `308207b2300b0609608648016503040312038207a100`, then the 1952-byte key); anything else is
   554	  `KEY_FORMAT` (RFC 9881 §§2, 4). Its fingerprint is §3.3's `spki-sha256`, unchanged. Key-use assumption (K-R) of
   555	  the supplement replaces PROOF (K) for this slot; the supplement carries both definitions.
   556	- *Consequence to record:* `common.alg` is one value, equal across slots (§0), naming the pair in slot order.
   557	- Vectors: `vectors/hybrid-golden.json` and `vectors/hybrid-reject.json`; agreement run and differential fuzz as in
   558	  PROOF §5 (supplement §8).
   559	- **Branch recorded:** hybrid, 2026-10-16 (A9.6).
   560	
   561	### 5.3 Verification uses the committed bytes, and only those
   562	
   563	The verifier first obtains the ordered tuple `(F_kms, S_kms, F_gpg, S_gpg)`, by either route below. The two are
   564	the same by PROOF Lemma 7.
   565	- decode `A_sig`; or
   566	- encode the presented tuple into `A_sig`.
   567	
   568	It checks that `SHA256(A_sig)` is the digest the OTS evidence attests. It then verifies **each committed `S` over
   569	its committed `F` under its slot's key**, using exactly those bytes.
   570	
   571	The verifier never does any of these:
   572	- verify a separately selected, re-serialized, normalized or reconstructed signature;
   573	- accept a receipt whose presented frames or signatures differ in any byte from the committed tuple
   574	  (`RECEIPT_ANCHOR_MISMATCH`), even when the presented signature is itself valid (negative controls XR-10 to
   575	  XR-14);
   576	- accept a frame-only anchor.
   577	
   578	The vectors exercise the following verification-stage codes. They are reported after every codec code:
   579	- `KEY_MISMATCH`: the presented public key does not hash to its slot's manifest fingerprint;
   580	- `SIG_INVALID`: a committed signature does not verify (XR-16, signatures swapped between slots, anchor rebuilt);
   581	- `UNVERIFIABLE` (`ALG_UNKNOWN`): the algorithm id is well-formed but is not the installation's catalog-supported
   582	  id. Being codec-known (a row of §5.2's table) is not being policy-supported: under the hybrid branch the
   583	  classical id is codec-known and still `UNVERIFIABLE` (V1-PROFILE §1, "Codec-known versus policy-supported").
   584	  [OWNER 2026-10-19, Levadura contested 5] *(Was: "the algorithm id is not registered", which used "registered" in
   585	  two senses.)*
   586	
   587	These codes belong to the signature verification profile (P3), not to P8.
   588	
   589	### 5.4 Issue order (finding 6)
   590	
   591	1. Fix the manifest and the common metadata, including `declared_issue_time`.
   592	2. Build `F_kms` and `F_gpg`.
   593	3. Produce one detached signature per slot over its frame.
   594	4. Verify both signatures over their frames under the slot keys, using the exact bytes to be committed.
   595	5. **Freeze** `S_kms` and `S_gpg`. Any serialization step, such as rule O re-framing, happens before this
   596	   point. Nothing changes a signature byte after it.
   597	6. Build `A_sig` and stamp `SHA256(A_sig)` with OpenTimestamps: **one** stamp per receipt (R-3.42).
   598	7. Obtain and validate the confirmation and time evidence.
   599	8. Ship the complete receipt.
   600	
   601	- A signature never includes its own eventual anchor time or proof.
   602	- A retry that changes any frame or signature byte needs a fresh stamp, and it obeys the existing reissue and

