   620	## 7. DNS key-publication record
   621	
   622	C4(i) needs a DNSSEC-authenticated record that binds V1's two keys to D. Neither the spec nor wamason.com has one
   623	yet (spike report; R-4.26 says only "DNSSEC records publishing the fingerprints").
   624	
   625	**Owner name:** `_tessera-v1.<D>`, where D is `common.issuer`.
   626	- The verifier rebuilds this name from the signed `issuer` and compares it, label by label and ignoring ASCII
   627	  case, with the validated RRset's owner name. Anything else gives `DNS_OWNER`.
   628	- *Reason:* the owner name itself is then the C4 owner-name check (G-21/M2). An underscore label cannot collide
   629	  with a hostname, and the `-v1` suffix lets a later profile publish next to it.
   630	
   631	**Type:** TXT. **RRset:** exactly **one** TXT record at the owner name (`DNS_RRSET` if there are zero or more).
   632	**RDATA:** exactly **one** character-string (`DNS_STRINGS` otherwise). If a string's length byte runs past the
   633	RDATA, the result is `DNS_RDATA`.
   634	- *Reason:* one string removes every question about how several strings concatenate. A provider that splits a
   635	  value is non-conforming for V1.
   636	- Every canonical value is **exactly 255 bytes**, the limit for one string (DNS-02): each field has a fixed length
   637	  and, since 2026-10-12, every field is mandatory. That leaves no room for another field. A later field needs a new
   638	  record version.
   639	
   640	**Value grammar** (ABNF; the canonical form is the only accepted form):
   641	
   642	```
   643	value      = "v=tessera1" ";profile=tessera-v1-strict" ";kms=" 64LHEX ";gpg=" 64LHEX
   644	             ";issuance-not-before=" utc ";issuance-not-after=" utc
   645	LHEX       = %x30-39 / %x61-66                              ; lowercase hex only
   646	utc        = 4DIGIT "-" 2DIGIT "-" 2DIGIT "T" 2DIGIT ":" 2DIGIT ":" 2DIGIT "Z"
   647	             ; RFC 3339 UTC; a real Gregorian date and time, seconds 00-59, no fraction
   648	             ; years 0001-9999, proleptic Gregorian [OWNER 2026-10-19, G18]
   649	```
   650	
   651	- `kms` is the hex of §3.3's `spki-sha256`. `gpg` is the hex of §3.3's `gpg-sha256`.
   652	- `issuance-not-before` and `issuance-not-after` are both **mandatory** (since 2026-10-12; `issuance-not-before` was
   653	  optional before). `not-after` must be strictly later than `not-before` (`DNS_WINDOW`), and
   654	  `not-after − not-before` must be at most 90 days, 7 776 000 s (`DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH`; Amendment 9's single V1
   655	  window). §7.1 gives their meaning.
   656	- `utc` is exactly the grammar of `declared_issue_time` (§3.2), so the window and the declared time compare
   657	  without conversion. The record's previous grammar was a date only. A one-second boundary cannot be stated with
   658	  a date, so the window uses the same time grammar as the signed bytes.
   659	- The 2026-10-09 optional `not-before=<date>` is replaced by `issuance-not-before` and is now `DNS_FIELD_UNKNOWN`
   660	  (XD-29).
   661	- The value has no spaces and no trailing `;`. Field order is fixed. Bytes are printable ASCII from 0x21 to 0x7E.
   662	
   663	Example (DNS-02):
   664	
   665	```
   666	v=tessera1;profile=tessera-v1-strict;kms=5cd252fb…7cd3;gpg=5ecd7121…8fc3;issuance-not-before=2026-10-12T00:00:00Z;issuance-not-after=2027-01-10T00:00:00Z
   667	```
   668	
   669	**Rejection order** (the first that applies):
   670	1. `DNS_OWNER`
   671	2. `DNS_RRSET`
   672	3. `DNS_RDATA`
   673	4. `DNS_STRINGS`
   674	5. `DNS_SYNTAX`:
   675	   - a byte outside 0x21–0x7E;
   676	   - a field that is not `key=value`, with key `[a-z][a-z-]*` and a nonempty value containing no `=`;
   677	   - an empty field.
   678	6. `DNS_VERSION`: the first field is not `v=tessera1`, or `v` appears again.
   679	7. `DNS_FIELD_UNKNOWN`: an extra field.
   680	8. `DNS_SLOT_DUPLICATE` (`kms` or `gpg` appears twice) or `DNS_FIELD_DUPLICATE` (another field appears twice).
   681	9. `DNS_SLOT_MISSING`.
   682	10. `DNS_FIELD_MISSING` (`profile`, `issuance-not-before` or `issuance-not-after`).
   683	11. `DNS_ORDER`.
   684	12. `DNS_PROFILE`.
   685	13. `DNS_VALUE`: the hex or a time fails its grammar.
   686	14. `DNS_WINDOW`: `issuance-not-after` ≤ `issuance-not-before`.
   687	15. `DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH`: `issuance-not-after − issuance-not-before` > 90 days (7 776 000 s).
   688	
   689	**Why the record is unambiguous** (outside PROOF.md's frame domain):
   690	- The accepted values form a regular language. Every field begins with a fixed literal key, and every value
   691	  comes from an alphabet without `;` or `=`, so splitting on `;` and then on the first `=` recovers the fields
   692	  uniquely.
   693	- The field order is fixed, and every value has exactly one spelling: lowercase hex, and `YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ`.
   694	  So the map from (kms, gpg, issuance-not-before, issuance-not-after) to value strings is injective, and its
   695	  inverse is the parse. `:` is outside the separators, and `DNS_WINDOW` and `DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH` are checks on parsed
   696	  values, so none of them affects the split.
   697	- One RR with one character-string removes concatenation ambiguity. The RDATA length byte delimits the string.
   698	- The owner name is compared as a label sequence, so `_tessera-v1.wamason.com` cannot match
   699	  `_tessera-v1.wamason.com.evil.example` or `tessera-v1.wamason.com` (XD-12 to XD-14).
   700	- The value begins `v=` (0x76), while every tagged string in §2 begins with a byte from 0x11 to 0x1d. So a TXT
   701	  value can never be a V1 frame, and a frame can never be a TXT value.
   702	
   703	### 7.1 Pre-committed issuance window (owner decision 2026-10-12; single 90-day window per Amendment 9)
   704	
   705	The key-publication record carries the issuance window. For V1 there is one window, of at most 90 days
   706	(`DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH`), with no overlapping successor: before it ends, either a rotation mechanism is registered or
   707	issuance stops (Amendment 9, A9.6). It is the same canonical value in both channels: the DNS
   708	TXT record, and the git key-assertion blob (§7.2). The same window value is authenticated by the anchored git
   709	assertion and by DNSSEC at the snapshot's own anchored time. Its pre-commitment is **chain order**: the git
   710	assertion's selected anchor height plus 5 is lower than the receipt's selected anchor height, so the window was
   711	confirmed at k = 6 in the chain before the receipt was anchored (A9 C4; V1-PROFILE §6.3, `GIT_NOT_PRECOMMITTED`).
   712	That is chain order, not UTC (A2.1), and DNSSEC evidence establishes no publication before issue (A9 C4). [OWNER
   713	2026-10-19, G02 CORRECT; Levadura C1] *(Was: "The window is therefore fixed in DNSSEC-authenticated and anchored
   714	authority evidence before any receipt that relies on it", which overstated both channels.)*
   715	
   716	**Verifier rule (C4).** A receipt passes C4 only if both of these hold:
   717	- `issuance-not-before` ≤ `declared_issue_time`;
   718	- **T ≤ `issuance-not-after`**, where T is the header time of the qualifying attestation at the **lowest block
   719	  height** in the presented OTS proof (A9 C5; A10.5's stages; V1-PROFILE §7.1). Header times are not monotone, so
   720	  selection is by height, never by least header time. [OWNER 2026-10-19, r2-contra; Levadura contested 1] *(Was:
   721	  "the time of the earliest verified attestation", A9's pre-2026-10-14 wording.)*
   722	
   723	Otherwise the verdict is `INVALID`, with reason `ISSUANCE_WINDOW`. Both bounds are inclusive: W-01 is
   724	T = not-after, W-02 is declared time = not-before. XW-01 is T = not-after + 1 s, and XW-02 is a declared time
   725	1 s early.
   726	
   727	*Rationale, recorded.*
   728	- **A surviving or stolen key cannot mint a receipt that passes with T after the window.** Keys may outlive the
   729	  service's exit, or be stolen. Even signing capability for every required slot cannot produce a receipt that
   730	  passes with verified T after `issuance-not-after`, under continuing anchor, history and authority assumptions. A fresh mint's `A_sig` (§5) can only
   731	  be stamped at or after the moment it exists, and the anchor prevents claiming an earlier T. So every receipt
   732	  whose verified T is after the window fails (XW-03). This is a statement in chain time, not in UTC (see the
   733	  residual below).
   734	  - A compromise *before* `not-after` is not covered. Within the window, a thief mints receipts that pass, subject
   735	    to the historical-security caveats of CODEX-REVIEW findings 7 and 8.
   736	- **Key-format expiries are not relied on.**
   737	  - OpenPGP key-expiration subpackets live in a self-signature. Any keyholder, a thief included, can re-issue that
   738	    self-signature to extend them.
   739	  - A raw SPKI key, such as a KMS ML-DSA key, carries no expiry at all.
   740	  - The window is instead fixed in authority evidence that the keyholder alone cannot rewrite after the fact.
   741	    Rewriting it means re-publishing the record, which is a new authority event with its own evidence times.
   742	
   743	*Residual: chain time (A2.1).*
   744	- T is the **header-time-derived T as registered**. The verifier compares it with `issuance-not-after` directly,
   745	  with no margin added or subtracted. That keeps one registered meaning of T.
   746	- A Bitcoin header's timestamp is not an exact UTC clock. Consensus constrains it only loosely: it must exceed the
   747	  median of the previous eleven blocks, and nodes accept it up to about two hours ahead of their own clock.
   748	- In the direction that matters here, a stamp actually made **after** `not-after` can carry a header time at or
   749	  before `not-after`. Its receipt passes. A2.1 registers no finite bound on that backward skew, and neither ε nor S
   750	  is one. So what this rule guarantees is "no receipt passes with verified T after `not-after`". Translating that
   751	  into a finite UTC cutoff needs an additional, explicit backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish
   752	  (Codex review of Amendment 9, finding 2; an earlier draft of this paragraph claimed such a bound).
   753	- The opposite error, an honest stamp made just before `not-after` getting a later header time and failing, is
   754	  the safe direction.
   755	- *Issuer invariant:* an attempt whose T would exceed `not-after` is not shipped. The stop margin that implements
   756	  it is an operating number, not part of this encoding (`docs/V1-OPERATIONS-RUNBOOK.md`). The verdict reports the
   757	  comparison with T as registered.
   758	
   759	### 7.2 Git channel: what its OTS stamp must cover (finding 9; added 2026-10-12)
   760	
   761	- **The blob's content.** Since the issuance-window decision (§7.1), the git key-assertion blob carries the same
   762	  canonical value as the TXT record, so both channels assert one byte string, window included.
   763	- **What P8 does not fix.** The blob's path, and any byte framing around the value: for example, whether a
   764	  trailing LF is allowed. Those are not P8's.
   765	- **What P8 fixes here.** The blob's anchor target.
   766	
   767	- **The stamp targets the signed commit's object id.** That commit's tree contains the assertion blob at the
   768	  profile path (V1-PROFILE §6.3, `tessera-v1/key-assertion`), and it carries a `gpgsig` header that verifies under
   769	  the repository key. Whether this commit introduced the blob is not checked (V1-PROFILE §15.4). [OWNER 2026-10-19,
   770	  r2-contra; Levadura C4] *(Was: "That commit adds the assertion blob".)*
   771	- These stamp targets are **not acceptable**:
   772	  - the tree;
   773	  - the blob;
   774	  - an unsigned commit, including the unsigned payload that the commit signature covers.
   775	- *Why, from git's documented object format:*
   776	  - gitformat-signature(5) (git 2.43.0, "COMMIT SIGNATURES") gives a commit signature's payload as the commit
   777	    object, embedded as the header entry `gpgsig`.
   778	  - gitglossary(7) says an object "is uniquely identified by the SHA-1 of its contents".
   779	  - So a signed commit's id is computed over the object **including** its `gpgsig` header. The signature lies
   780	    inside the anchored bytes.
   781	  - A stamp over the tree or blob covers no signature. A stamp over the unsigned commit is the receipt's own
   782	    pre-stage-then-sign gap again.
   783	- *Checked 2026-10-12* (git 2.43.0, GnuPG 2.4.4, scratch repository):
   784	  - the signed commit's id equals `SHA-1("commit " ‖ decimal size ‖ 0x00 ‖ raw object)`;
   785	  - the raw object contains the `gpgsig` header;
   786	  - `git hash-object -t commit` reproduces the id;
   787	  - the unsigned commit with the same tree had a different id, while its tree id was identical.
   788	- **Matching the repository's convention.** This repository stamps a file `timestamps/<id>` holding the commit's
   789	  lowercase hex id and LF, after the commit exists. For a signed commit, that is this target.
   790	- **What the verifier does.** It:
   791	  1. recomputes the id from the presented raw commit object;
   792	  2. verifies `gpgsig` over the object without that header;
   793	  3. follows the commit to its tree and then to the blob;
   794	  4. checks that the stamped bytes name that id.
   795	- The git signature is ASCII-armored inside the commit header. §5.2's binary rule applies to receipt slots only.
   796	- **Residual, already recorded.** Two parts are SHA-1:
   797	  - the commit id itself;
   798	  - the commit → tree → blob linkage.
   799	
   800	  Both are in the dependency manifest (V1-DERIVATION's bundle contents and custody residual) and outside P8's
   801	  SHA-256 scope (PROOF (H)). Anchoring a signed commit puts its signature inside the anchored bytes. It does not
   802	  turn the SHA-1 links into SHA-256 bindings.
