    62	
    63	## 1. The accepted domain, exactly
    64	
    65	Let **V** be the set of JSON values in E§1.1:
    66	- `null`, `true` and `false`;
    67	- finite binary64 numbers other than −0;
    68	- strings of Unicode scalar values that contain no noncharacter, no code point unassigned in Unicode 16.0.0, and are
    69	  in NFC;
    70	- arrays;
    71	- objects with pairwise distinct names.
    72	
    73	Two values are **equal** when they are equal as mathematical structures:
    74	- numbers are equal as doubles;
    75	- strings are equal as scalar sequences;
    76	- arrays are equal as sequences;
    77	- objects are equal as finite maps from names to values. Member order is not part of an object.
    78	
    79	That is the sense in which injectivity is claimed. Two JSON *texts* that differ only in spelling denote one value,
    80	and E§1.2 says why that is intended.
    81	
    82	The accepted sets are:
    83	
    84	- **Payloads.** For a tag `t`, `Pay(t) = { JCS(v) : v ∈ V, Schema_t(v), depth(v) ≤ 64, |JCS(v)| ≤ 2^24 }`. Here
    85	  `Schema_attest` is E§3.2 together with E§3.3, and `Schema_manifest` is E§4.
    86	- **Frames.** `Frm(t) = { frame(t, p) : p ∈ Pay(t) }` for `t ∈ {attest, manifest}`, where
    87	  `frame(t, p) = [|t|] ‖ t ‖ 00 01 ‖ u32(|p|) ‖ p`.
    88	- **Signature slots.** For an algorithm id `a` and a slot `i ∈ {0 (kms), 1 (gpg)}`, `Sig(a, i)` is the set of
    89	  byte strings accepted by the E§5.2 rule for `(a, i)`. It is always a set of **nonempty** strings of length at
    90	  most `cap(a, i)`.
    91	  - Rule O: exactly one legacy-header v4 signature packet, with every inner length consumed exactly.
    92	  - Rule R: the strings of the parameter set's exact signature length.
    93	  - Opaque: every string of 1 to 65536 bytes.
    94	- **Anchor preimages.** `Anc = { A(F0, S0, F1, S1) : F_i ∈ Frm(attest), S_i ∈ Sig(alg(F_i), i) }`, where `alg(F)`
    95	  is the `common.alg` of `F`'s payload and
    96	  `A(F0, S0, F1, S1) = [29] ‖ "tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig" ‖ 02 ‖ u32|F0| ‖ F0 ‖ u32|S0| ‖ S0 ‖ u32|F1| ‖ F1 ‖ u32|S1| ‖ S1`.
    97	  The withdrawn frame-only construction (tag `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor`) is not in the domain. Its tag is not in
    98	  T.
    99	- **Snapshot preimages.** `Snp = { S(s) : 1 ≤ |s| ≤ 2^24 }`, where `S(s) = [26] ‖ "tessera-v1/dnssec-snapshot" ‖ u32|s| ‖ s`.
   100	- **Receipts.** A tuple `(F_M, F_0, S_0, F_1, S_1, A)` is an *accepted receipt* (codec level) when all of the
   101	  following hold:
   102	  - `F_M ∈ Frm(manifest)` decodes to a manifest `M`;
   103	  - `F_0` and `F_1` are in `Frm(attest)` and decode to `(C_0, fp_0)` and `(C_1, fp_1)`;
   104	  - `fp_0 = M.key_fp` and `fp_1 = M.sset[0]`;
   105	  - `JCS(C_0) = JCS(C_1)`;
   106	  - `C_0.manifest_sha256 = hex(SHA256(F_M))`, and `C_0.issuer` and `C_0.alg` equal those of `M`;
   107	  - `S_i ∈ Sig(C_0.alg, i)` and `A = A(F_0, S_0, F_1, S_1)`.
   108	
   109	  The receipt's structure is `(M, C, S_kms, S_gpg)`. It is **not** a function of `(M, C)` alone. The signer's
   110	  choices are inside `S`: the nonce, the creation time and the unhashed area. So one `(M, C)` has many accepted
   111	  receipts, and P8 claims nothing about which one an issuer produces.
   112	
   113	The decoders in E§1.4, E§3.1, E§4, E§5 and E§6 accept exactly these sets and reject everything else with the
   114	stated codes. Lemma 3 shows this for payloads, and Lemmas 5–8 (with 7a) for the rest.
   115	
   116	## 2. Assumptions
   117	
   118	- **(N) Number formatting.** For every finite double `d`, `ES(d)` (ECMA-262 Number::toString, used by RFC 8785
   119	  §3.2.2.3) is a decimal literal whose round-half-even nearest double is `d`. Decimal-to-double conversion is
   120	  correctly rounded (IEEE 754). The ECMAScript specification requires both. The implementations rely on them
   121	  through `rfc8785` (A) and through `repr` and `float` (B).
   122	- **(H) SHA-256 collision resistance.** This is used **only** in Theorem part (iv), for the
   123	  hash-carried bindings and the anchor digest, which since 2026-10-12 also carries the exact signatures. Parts
   124	  (i)–(iii) are unconditional.
   125	  - **SHA-256 is the only hash function V1's encoding relies on.** Both fingerprint slots are SHA-256: of the SPKI
   126	    for kms, and of the OpenPGP v4 key-packet body for gpg.
   127	  - The git channel's object linkage is SHA-1, but that belongs to C4's dependency manifest, not P8 (E§7.2).
   128	  - An OpenPGP signature as GnuPG emits it carries OpenPGP's own SHA-1 issuer fingerprint in a subpacket. Those
   129	    bytes are committed in `A_sig` like the rest, but no V1 check reads them.
   130	- **(U) Unicode 16.0.0 tables.** These define V's string conditions. They bear on what is accepted, never on
   131	  injectivity.
   132	- **(K) Key use.** Each V1 slot key signs only V1 tagged strings, as OpenPGP type-0x00 documents. The repository
   133	  key and the DNSSEC keys are other keys. This is used only in Remark 10.
   134	
   135	P8 assumes nothing about whether signatures can be forged. That is P1 and P3. Nor does P8 assume anything about
   136	time: what an OTS proof establishes about when `SHA256(A_sig)` existed belongs to C5 and its chain-time
   137	residuals. Theorem (iv) states how the three compose, and keeps them apart.
   138	
   139	---
   140	
   141	## 3. Lemmas
   142	
   143	**Lemma 1 (strings).** Let `E(s)` be RFC 8785's escaping of a string `s` of scalar values (E§1.3), and let `Dec`
   144	be RFC 8259 string decoding, which pairs surrogate escapes. Then for every string `s` in V, decoding
   145	`"` E(s) `"` returns exactly `s`, consumes exactly those bytes, and stops at the closing quote.
   146	
   147	*Proof.* `E` maps each scalar `c` on its own:
   148	- `"` becomes `\"`;
   149	- `\` becomes `\\`;
   150	- a control character `c < 0x20` becomes its two-character escape where RFC 8785 defines one, and `\u00xx`
   151	  otherwise;
   152	- every other `c` becomes its UTF-8 encoding, which is defined because `s` contains no surrogates.
   153	
   154	None of the outputs contains an unescaped `"`. Every output that starts with `\` is a complete escape that `Dec`
   155	maps back to `c`. Every other output is a raw scalar `≥ 0x20`, other than `"` and `\`, which `Dec` copies.

