   160	**Lemma 2 (numbers).** Let `d` be a finite double other than −0. Then:
   161	- (a) `ES(d)` matches the RFC 8259 number grammar;
   162	- (b) parsing `ES(d)` gives `d`;
   163	- (c) `ES(d)` passes the value-preserving rule E§1.2;
   164	- (d) in `JCS(v)`, `ES(d)` is followed by `,`, `]`, `}` or the end of input.
   165	
   166	*Proof.*
   167	- (a) `ES` produces only the forms `digits`, `digits.digits`, `0.0…digits` and `d[.ddd]e±n`, with an optional
   168	  leading `-`. Integer parts have no leading zero, except the single `0` of `0.`. All of these are grammatical.
   169	- (b) This is (N).
   170	- (c) Rule 1: `ES(d)` is never a negative zero, because `d ≠ −0` and `ES(+0) = "0"`. Rule 2: the nearest double
   171	  to `ES(d)` is `d` by (b), which is finite. Rule 3: `ES(d)` is the literal being tested, so its value equals the
   172	  value of `ES` of its nearest double, which is `ES(d)` again.
   173	- (d) JCS puts a number only before a separator or a closing bracket, or at the end of the text. None of those can
   174	  extend a number token, so a maximal-munch number scan over `JCS(v)` stops exactly at the end of `ES(d)`. ∎
