   268	  `gpg` to `M.sset[0]`, which have different prefixes.
   269	
   270	A swapped presentation fails `RECEIPT_SLOT`. So does the same frame presented twice (XR-01, XR-02). A splice that
   271	changes `C` in one slot fails `RECEIPT_COMMON_MISMATCH` (XR-03). ∎
   272	
   273	*Why the two frames share one tag.* A5 §A5.4 registers that object type is one of the six fields that are equal
   274	across slots. So separation between the two signers' frames is done by the fingerprint slot and the manifest's
   275	slot order, not by the tag. Lemma 6(c) is that separation.
   276	
   277	**Lemma 7a (signature slots).** For every `(a, i)`, the slot decoder accepts a byte string `s` of declared slot
   278	length `n` if and only if `s ∈ Sig(a, i)` and `|s| = n`. When it accepts, it returns `s` itself. The signature's
   279	end is fixed twice, and the two must agree:
   280	- by the slot's `u32` length;
   281	- by the signature's own extent: rule O's packet header, rule R's fixed length, or, for opaque, the slot length.
   282	
   283	*Proof.*
   284	- The slot length `n` is read first.
   285	  - `n = 0` gives `SIG_EMPTY`.
   286	  - `n > cap(a, i)` gives `LENGTH_CAP`.
   287	  - Otherwise exactly `n` bytes are taken.
   288	- **Rule O.** Byte 0 must be `0x88` or `0x89`. It fixes the width of the length field (1 or 2 bytes), and the
   289	  shortest-form condition makes that width a function of the body length `m`. So the header is
   290	  `h ∈ {2, 3}` bytes, and the packet ends at `h + m`.
   291	  - `h + m < n` gives `TRAILING`. This includes a second packet.
   292	  - `h + m > n` gives `TRUNCATED`.
   293	  - Inside the body, every field's extent is fixed by bytes before it: four fixed bytes; `u16` and the hashed
   294	    area; `u16` and the unhashed area; two bytes; and then each MPI's `u16` bit count with ⌈bits/8⌉ bytes.
   295	  - Each subpacket area must be consumed exactly by subpackets whose lengths are in shortest form. The body must
   296	    end exactly after the second MPI.
   297	  - Anything else gives `SIG_FORMAT`.
   298	  - So the accepted set is exactly rule O's, and no accepted string has a proper prefix or extension that is also
   299	    accepted at the same slot length.
   300	- **Rule R.** The accepted strings are those of the exact parameter-set length.
   301	- **Opaque.** The accepted strings are those of the slot length.
   302	
   303	In every case the decoder returns the bytes it took, unchanged. It normalizes nothing: no re-framing, no low-s,
   304	no subpacket edits. ∎
   305	
   306	*Remark.* The decoder returns `s` unchanged, so the codec is injective on signatures trivially. What Lemma 7a
   307	adds is that a signature's boundaries are unambiguous, and that the accepted set is pinned. That second fact keeps
   308	an encoder from choosing among serializations, and it makes two verifiers agree on rejection.
   309	
   310	**Lemma 7 (anchor preimage; revised 2026-10-12).** `A(·,·,·,·)` is injective on ordered tuples
   311	`(F0, S0, F1, S1)` with `F_i ∈ Frm(attest)` and `S_i ∈ Sig(alg(F_i), i)`, and
   312	`decode_anchor(A(F0, S0, F1, S1)) = ((F0, S0), (F1, S1))`. Every byte string that `decode_anchor` accepts equals
   313	`A` of its decoded tuple. In particular, `A` of a tuple differs from `A` of any other tuple. That covers each of
   314	these changes:
   315	- the slots swapped;
   316	- the signatures swapped between slots;
   317	- any signature byte replaced;
   318	- one valid signature over a frame replaced by another valid signature over the same frame.
   319	
   320	*Proof.* The header comes first (Lemma 4). Then come the fixed count byte `02`, and for each slot `i` in order:
   321	1. the length `u32 L_i`, then exactly `L_i` bytes, decoded as a whole frame (Lemma 5). That decode determines
   322	   `alg(F_i)`;
   323	2. the length `u32 M_i`, then exactly `M_i` bytes, decoded by the rule for `(alg(F_i), i)` (Lemma 7a).
   324	
   325	Then the end of input. Each boundary is fixed by bytes already read. The choice of signature rule depends only on
   326	the frame just decoded and the slot position, both already read. No later byte influences an earlier boundary or
   327	rule. So the decoder is a left inverse of `A` on the domain, and `A` is injective. Conversely, an accepted string
   328	is rebuilt exactly by `A` from its decoded tuple, since each field is copied verbatim and each length prefix is
   329	the length of the field after it. ∎
   330	
   331	*Corollary (no fallback).* The withdrawn frame-only preimage begins with `header("tessera-v1/receipt-anchor")`.
   332	That tag is not in T, so the decoder rejects it at its header (Lemma 4, `TAG_UNKNOWN`), before any length is read
   333	(XA-09, XR-09).
   334	
   335	**Lemma 8 (snapshot preimage).** `S(·)` is a bijection from snapshot bodies of 1 to 2^24 bytes onto `Snp`. The
   336	proof is Lemma 5's with an opaque body.
   337	
   338	**Lemma 9 (the OpenPGP hash input splits uniquely; narrowed 2026-10-19, P8F-01).** For a v4 OpenPGP signature of
   339	type 0x00 over document `F`, the hashed input is `F ‖ T`, where `T = 04 00 pa ha ‖ u16 h ‖ sub ‖ 04 FF ‖ u32(6 + h)`
   340	(E§3.5) and `|sub| = h`. The map `(F, T) ↦ F ‖ T` is injective over valid trailers: if `F ‖ T = F' ‖ T'` for valid
   341	trailers `T` and `T'`, then `F = F'` and `T = T'`. In particular, for the trailer `T` that a signature packet fixes,
   342	`F ‖ T = F' ‖ T` implies `F = F'`.
   343	
   344	*Proof.* A valid trailer ends with `u32(6 + h)`, where `6 + h` is the length of its hashed header `04 00 pa ha ‖ u16
   345	h ‖ sub`. The whole trailer is that header, the two bytes `04 FF` and the four length bytes, so `|T| = 12 + h`:
   346	the final u32 plus six. Two decompositions `F ‖ T = F' ‖ T'` share their last four bytes, so `6 + h = 6 + h'` and
   347	`|T| = |T'|`. Both trailers are then the same-length suffix of one string, so `T = T'` and `F = F'`. (Corrected
   348	2026-10-19, P8F-05: the final u32 is the hashed-header length, not the trailer's own length; the split is the
   349	final u32 plus six.)
   350	
   351	Type 0x01 is excluded because text mode rewrites line-ending bytes, and those bytes occur in frame headers and
   352	lengths. ∎
   353	
   354	*What Lemma 9 does not say (NARROW-CLAIM, P8F-01).* Until 2026-10-19 this lemma also claimed that if one signature
   355	packet verifies for `F` and for `F'`, then under (H) `F' = F`. That does not follow: verification does not
   356	establish that the two hashes are equal. The falsification review built two distinct accepted attestation frames
   357	whose SHA-256 digests of `F ‖ T` differ (agreeing only in OpenPGP's left 16 bits) and one rule-O packet that
   358	verifies over both under one chosen P-256 key (`prototype/p8-falsify/lemma9-witness.json`). Whether one signature
   359	can verify over two different documents is a property of the signature scheme and of how the key was generated
   360	(P1, P3), not of the encoding. P8 does not claim it. Theorem (ii) uses only the split above; Theorem (iv) never
   361	used Lemma 9, and (iv)(c) already leaves verification to P1 and P3.
   362	
   363	**Remark 10 (no confusion with foreign formats).** Every V1 tagged string begins with a byte in 0x11–0x1d, which
   364	is below 0x20. Each of the following begins with a printable byte:
   365	- a git commit object (`tree …`, 0x74);
   366	- a TXT value of E§7 (`v=`, 0x76);
   367	- RFC 8785 text (`{`, `[`, `"`, a digit, `-`, `t`, `f` or `n`).
   368	
   369	So no V1 tagged string is byte-equal to any of them: a V1 signed document is never also a commit, a DNS value or
   370	RFC 8785 text. (K) keeps the slot keys off those formats. [OWNER 2026-10-19, P8F-01, NARROW-CLAIM] *(Was: "With (K)
   371	this is not needed, but it means that a key reused by mistake still could not have one of its V1 signatures read as
   372	a signature over a commit or a DNS value, or the reverse." Different documents do not by themselves prevent one
   373	signature from verifying over both; that is a signature-scheme property (Lemma 9's note), not P8's.)*
   374	
   375	**Lemma 11 (DNS record).** The unambiguity of the E§7 record is argued in E§7 itself, because it lies outside the
   376	frame domain. In summary:
   377	- the accepted values form a fixed-order regular language over alphabets that exclude the separators, so parsing
   378	  inverts encoding;
   379	  - this includes, since 2026-10-12, the mandatory `issuance-not-before` and `issuance-not-after` (the first was
   380	    optional until the Amendment 9 disposition), whose values are `YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ`, one spelling per instant;
   381	  - `DNS_WINDOW` (not-after ≤ not-before) and `DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH` (not-after − not-before > 90 days) are checks on
   382	    parsed values and play no part in the split;
   383	- one RR with one character-string removes concatenation choices;
   384	- the owner name is compared as a sequence of labels, without regard to ASCII case.
   385	
