   388	## 4. Theorem (P8 for `tessera-v1-strict`)
   389	
   390	Let the accepted sets be as in §1.
   391	
   392	- **(i) Injectivity.** These maps are injective:
   393	  - `canonical = JCS` on V;
   394	  - `frame(t, ·)` on `Pay(t)`, and so `v ↦ frame(t, JCS(v))` on the schema-valid values;
   395	  - `A` on ordered (frame, signature) tuples `(F_kms, S_kms, F_gpg, S_gpg)` (Lemmas 7, 7a);
   396	  - `S` on snapshot bodies.
   397	
   398	  Each decoder is the exact inverse of its encoder on the accepted set: `Dec ∘ Enc = id` on accepted structures,
   399	  and `Enc ∘ Dec = id` on accepted bytes. *(Lemmas 3, 5, 7, 7a, 8.)*
   400	
   401	  So the map from an accepted receipt's structure `(M, C, S_kms, S_gpg)` to `(F_M, F_kms, F_gpg, A_sig)` is
   402	  injective outright:
   403	  - `F_M` determines `M`;
   404	  - `F_kms` determines `C`;
   405	  - `A_sig` determines `S_kms` and `S_gpg`, byte for byte.
   406	
   407	  The receipt is not determined by `(M, C)` alone (§1).
   408	
   409	- **(ii) Boundary-unambiguity.** In every accepted byte string, each field's extent is a function of the bytes
   410	  before it. The end of input is required to fall exactly where the last length prefix says. The JSON payload is

