 succeeded in 0ms:
   409	- **(ii) Boundary-unambiguity.** In every accepted byte string, each field's extent is a function of the bytes
   410	  before it. The end of input is required to fall exactly where the last length prefix says. The JSON payload is
   411	  delimited by the frame's length, never by its own syntax. A signature's extent is fixed by its slot length, and
   412	  it must agree with the signature's own extent. *(Lemmas 5, 7, 7a, 8; Lemma 9 for the split of the OpenPGP hash
   413	  input into frame and trailer.)*
   414	
   415	- **(iii) Type and domain separation.** The accepted sets `Frm(attest)`, `Frm(manifest)`, `Anc` and `Snp` are
   416	  pairwise disjoint (Lemma 4). A decoder for one construction rejects every member of the others (`TAG_MISMATCH`).
   417	  The withdrawn frame-only anchor layout is rejected by every decoder (`TAG_UNKNOWN`; Lemma 7's corollary).
   418	
   419	  Inside `Frm(attest)`, the `kms` and `gpg` slot frames are disjoint, each frame's slot is fixed by its own bytes,
   420	  and an accepted receipt's two frames are distinct and byte-identical on `JCS(C)` (Lemma 6).
   421	
   422	  The DNS value and foreign formats are disjoint from all of these (Remark 10).
   423	
   424	- **(iv) Computational binding, under (H)** *(revised 2026-10-12)*. Each of the following binds only under SHA-256
   425	  collision resistance.
   426	  - A frame's signed bytes determine the manifest frame, the specification, the conformance vectors and the
   427	    preamble, through their SHA-256 values.
   428	  - The OTS-stamped digest `SHA256(A_sig)` determines the ordered tuple `((F_kms, S_kms), (F_gpg, S_gpg))`,
   429	    through Lemma 7: the frames **and the exact signature bytes**, in slot order.
   430	
   431	  So two different such objects bound by one accepted frame or anchor digest yield a SHA-256 collision. This is
   432	  the only place P8 for V1 rests on a cryptographic assumption.
   433	
   434	  **Four statements, kept apart** (finding 10). P8 proves (a) and (b). (c) and (d) are not P8's, and P8 does not
   435	  claim them.
   436	  - **(a) Codec injectivity** (unconditional). `A_sig` decodes to exactly one tuple and is re-encoded from it
   437	    (Lemma 7). A different signature byte string, even a valid one over the same frame, gives a different
   438	    preimage.
   439	  - **(b) Digest binding** (under (H)). A digest determines that tuple. A second tuple with the same digest is a
   440	    SHA-256 collision.
   441	  - **(c) Unforgeability** (P1 and P3, not P8). Whether `S` verifies over `F` under the slot key is a separate
   442	    check. It is made on the committed bytes (E§5.3). Whether a verifying `S` was produced by the key holder
   443	    rests on the signature scheme's security and on key custody.
   444	  - **(d) Temporal composition** (C5, not P8). Suppose valid OTS and Bitcoin evidence shows that
   445	    `SHA256(A_sig)` existed by time T. Then by (b), the exact bytes `S_kms` and `S_gpg` existed by T, under that
   446	    evidence's own chain-time residuals.
   447	    - Hence a signature first produced after T, for example once a key is stolen or the algorithm broken, cannot
   448	      be substituted into this receipt while (H) and the temporal evidence hold.
   449	    - It does **not** follow that the signatures were produced honestly before T. That needs the historical
   450	      signing, custody and authority assumptions of CODEX-REVIEW findings 7 and 8, which V1 reports as not
   451	      evaluated.
   452	
   453	∎ (from the lemmas)
   454	
   455	**What is not claimed.** P8 does not claim any of the following:
   456	- that signatures are unforgeable, or that keys are bound to identities (P1, P3);
   457	- that a signature verifying over one document cannot also verify over another (Lemma 9's note; P1, P3)
   458	  [OWNER 2026-10-19, P8F-01];
   459	- that a stamp's time bound makes signing honest (iv)(d);
   460	- that the values have any meaning (A3 §A3.1);
   461	- that any value outside V is accepted. It is rejected with the code its first failing check names.
