   500	
   501	**Golden anchor digests.**
   502	- A-01 = R-01's anchor = `06caaf1a462b150f7f5b4a02691da1216259a5a080ad77dcfe1ffefd16ec64e4`. A-01 is 1932 bytes
   503	  and holds two 119-byte signatures. The frame-only A-01 of 2026-10-09 was `2d1e760f…4771427`.
   504	- A-02 (canary) = `1d9f006cfca4e80eb87bd88eb20944b8dba2e5256ffe996f38f9ab27cfd91583`.
   505	
   506	The output is deterministic: RFC 6979 nonces, and regeneration reproduces both files byte for byte.
   507	
   508	**Signatures.** The signatures are real ECDSA P-256 signatures in OpenPGP v4 packets.
   509	- The keys are public test keys: d = 1 (G, slot kms) and d = 2 (2G, slot gpg), as in K-01 and K-02.
   510	- The packet layout is the one GnuPG 2.4.4 emits.
   511	- A signs. A and B each verify with their own ECDSA code: A uses affine coordinates and B Jacobian, and each
   512	  builds its own OpenPGP hash input.
   513	- **External cross-check** (2026-10-12, by hand in a scratch GnuPG home, not part of the scripts). GnuPG 2.4.4
   514	  reported "Good signature" for SIG-01, SIG-02 and all five negative-control signatures: XR-10 (a second
   515	  nonce), XR-11 and XR-14 (`(r, n − s)`), XR-12 (an edited unhashed area) and XR-13 (signed five years later).
   516	  It reported "BAD signature" for a gpg-slot signature checked over the kms frame. A signature that GnuPG made
   517	  with its own fresh key also passes rule O.
   518	
   519	**Result** (2026-10-12; rerun after the 90-day window change, same day).
   520	- A self-checks every vector. Each negative control is asserted at generation: the presented signature verifies,
   521	  and its anchor digest differs from the stamped one.
   522	- B round-trips all **71/71** golden vectors. That includes re-parsing and re-verifying SIG-01 and SIG-02, and
   523	  verifying both committed signatures of R-01.
   524	- B rejects all **173/173** rejection vectors **with the same reason code as A**.
   525	  - B's window check uses its own days-from-civil arithmetic, not `datetime`.
   526	  - It matched `datetime` on 20,000 random instants (a manual check).
   527	- B independently confirms the **5/5** negative controls: the signature verifies, and the anchor check fails.
   528	- `vectors/fuzz.py` runs 60,000 seeded differential cases, and **there were 0 disagreements** in verdict, reason
   529	  code, canonical bytes or decoded anchor tuples.
   530	  - Stages 1 and 2 (40,000 cases: JSON and frames) are unchanged from 2026-10-09. Their verdict histogram
   531	    reproduces that run exactly.
   532	  - Stage 3 adds 20,000 composite-anchor cases: byte edits biased to the signature slots and length prefixes,
   533	    and structural substitutions, duplications, truncations, re-framings and swaps. Its verdicts: 3858 accepted;
   534	    the rest rejected across 13 reason codes, including `SIG_EMPTY`, `SIG_PACKET`, `SIG_FORMAT`, `LENGTH_CAP`,
   535	    `TRUNCATED` and `TRAILING`.
   536	- *Sensitivity check* (by hand, on scratch copies). Two planted defects in B were each caught by the agreement
   537	  run: accepting a non-minimal `0x89` header (XA-20), and skipping signature verification (XR-16).
   538	
   539	**Falsification pass repairs** (2026-10-19, P8F-02 to P8F-04). The review found three places where B did not match
   540	E§1: an uncaught `ValueError` on accepted literals whose exponent exceeds Python's integer digit limit (`0e` and 4301
   541	nines); `JSON_SYNTAX` for a BOM followed by invalid UTF-8, where E§1.4 requires `JSON_ENCODING`; and no size cap in
   542	the standalone `payload_value`. B now clamps exponents of more than 18 significant digits (no nonzero such literal
   543	is finite or nonzero as a double, and zero is decided first), decodes UTF-8 before the BOM check, and rejects a
   544	standalone payload above 2^24 bytes with `LENGTH_CAP`. The owner found the same three defects in A and repaired
   545	them the same way; A's old direct-decision bound (|exponent| > 100000) also wrongly rejected value-preserving
   546	literals such as `1` + 200000 zeros + `e-200000`, and now applies only above 18 exponent digits.
   547	`vectors/test_check.py` pins each reproduction for A and B. The rerun after the repair: B 71/71, 173/173 and 5/5
   548	baseline, 35/35, 63/63, 1/1 and 6/6 hybrid; A regenerates all four vector files byte for byte (checked in a
   549	scratch copy); `fuzz.py` 60,000 + 90,400 cases, 0 disagreements. No vector changed.
   550	
   551	The fuzz found one real defect in A before the 2026-10-09 vectors were frozen. A raised an uncaught `decimal`
   552	error on literals with exponents beyond about 10^18. A now decides those directly, and XC-31, XC-32 and C-10 pin
   553	the case.
