   131	## Choices made here (owner level; none changes a registered sentence)
   132	
   133	1. **One entry per document**, not per A9 concept, so that each entry has one edition and one digest. Multi-file
   134	   sources (Bitcoin Core, git) take the digest of a `SHA256SUMS` listing.
   135	2. **ECMA-262 10th edition (June 2019), section 7.1.12.1 NumberToString with Note 2**: the edition RFC 8785
   136	   itself cites.
   137	3. **Bitcoin Core v31.1** (commit 9be056a8…) as the reference for formats the profile restates in full.
   138	4. **git v2.56.0** (commit a0189536…) for `gitformat-signature` and `fsck.c`; Pro Git at progit2 a013e323….
   139	5. **GnuPG 2.4.4** for `gpgv`, because it is the version observed. R3/R6 have not pinned a version yet.
   140	6. **Errata**: verified RFC errata that touch a section used are adopted where they clarify (8785 #7920, 3110
   141	   #2811, 4034 #4552, 8259 #7600/#7603/#7383, 1035 #2130); NIST "potential updates" are not official changes, and
   142	   none changes a verification outcome. RFC 8017 and RFC 5280 errata were counted, not reviewed one by one (stated
   143	   in their entries).
   144	7. **Excluded**: FIPS 205 (cited in ENC rule R as a class, not used by the registered hybrid branch); ITU-T X.690
   145	   (ENC and the supplement fix the DER bytes exactly); Python, `cryptography` and other wheels (R6's release lock,
   146	   not normative standards).
   147	
   148	## Where the profile's section 9 / 15.6 text and what I found disagree
   149	
   150	Not edited here; for the owner.
   151	
   152	1. **Section 15.6 "The entry list is section 9's."** Section 9 names concepts (A9's eight) mixed with documents,
   153	   and its "Extra standards" list groups several RFCs per line. A per-document manifest cannot be "section 9's
   154	   list" literally. The mapping above is the correspondence.
   155	2. **Section 9's list omits standards the frozen text uses:** RFC 8017 (RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5, needed for algorithm-8
   156	   RRSIGs; RFC 5702 cites its predecessor RFC 3447); FIPS 202 (SHAKE256, called directly in ENC rule R); SP 800-186
   157	   (named in profile section 8, not in section 9); RFC 9881 (cited by ENC and the supplement; section 9 says only
   158	   "the supplement's SPKI encoding"); RFC 7493 and IEEE 754 (both in ENC section 1.1); RFC 6840 (updates RFC 4034
   159	   section 6.2, which section 6.1 uses); RFC 5280 (RFC 5652 alone does not verify the `.p7s` certificate path).
   160	   All are added.
   161	3. **"ECMA-262 Number::toString (pinned edition)"** (sections 9 and 10): no edition is named anywhere. In the
   162	   edition RFC 8785 cites (ES2019), the operation is called NumberToString (7.1.12.1); "Number::toString" is the
   163	   ES2020+ name (6.1.6.1.20). The output is the same. The profile's name and the pinned edition's name differ.
   164	4. **Section 15.6 has one `digest_sha256` per entry** and does not say what to hash when canonical bytes are many
   165	   files (Bitcoin Core, git, SHA-1DC) or when only an HTML and a PDF form exist (ECMA-262: PDF chosen; the HTML
   166	   digest is in `retrieval`). It also has no field for "informative" (RFC 9102, RFC 5480, BIP 144) or for
   167	   "distributed with the verifier versus retrieved" (item 15 asks for both). Both went into free text.
   168	5. **"The DNSSEC algorithms in the chain"** (A9, section 9 row 6) versus section 6.1's supported set {8, 13} with
   169	   DS {2, 4}. The manifest pins the supported set, which is wider than any one chain.
